[{:db/id 17592186195177 :answer-field/correct 17592186195180 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195182 :answer-field/correct 17592186195185 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181172"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/5c4ffbb64a754df9a6100fda3fd6eaf4a68f28ac59de4dfb716c2c8b1f2847ab.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195301 :answer-field/correct 17592186195306 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195289 :answer-field/correct 17592186195293 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186195295 :answer-field/correct 17592186195298 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181600"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/83ebd6f5d742a869b267a0cead4a9a94ae17fa534ca3e1cfddbb495c67390ecf.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "8ca276ea-f25a-5a0d-b4e2-f54ee1550527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "af7ab909-5668-5be9-9bcc-3f71e8264e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "44750a13-f5d9-5ed1-ba24-90bf45e702b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181694/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-181694/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-181694/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "f510c544-28bc-5a4f-9019-dae6f270165d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2f83ff-acd0-5c85-8a25-55554d6da5ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181694/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-181694/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-3/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0" :answer/id #uuid "d9514b47-883b-5f75-8351-7bda7ce95975" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "78a507d8-3547-5d06-9532-b0c9abda6de8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-181694/field-3/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0" :answer-field/choices ["q-181694/field-3/f022eff7ecaa4435243255d76a2a932e99db9e71ca2b89b05fafca75800709a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181694" :question/id #uuid "b4e1ca48-5085-5e98-9dc5-2438b5edfafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-181694" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 36}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-181694/field-1" "q-181694/field-2" "q-181694/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181694"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181999/field-1/5fc5100246d98d57a3a3e1d40181b00e6e8dff5d4a097ce2d7b7f6ae0b08578e" :answer/id #uuid "61da26c7-e591-5968-b270-3b14d8b86c1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181999/field-1/07d4d169afe54b72e6508fedd544e5d357fac0ff0f623249ab3b7f264f513fcf" :answer/id #uuid "fc636fc0-a884-5e6f-8e2f-eb0acc20b395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181999/field-1/62fc74d0eb6ece7bf9beb52d92d3e5dd4c591eb0e1df3eb95bec90a49e0fa322" :answer/id #uuid "e4895942-3164-5d01-80ce-c091c763efd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{C}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181999/field-1/a1f44d584f3d5c0539cb338e6289ad49d176231f52c1da87f334282351fe2472" :answer/id #uuid "86001284-c8ed-50a8-b244-67de7558bd6c" :answer/type :answer.